ify those  patients now we have better tools to  provide better prognostic information in  just about every aspect of medicine and  oncology is no exception we are never  really dealing with every individual  patient a context that has been  documented in a prospective randomized  study prospective randomized studies  always have very rigid inclusion and  exclusion criteria to guide us toward a  very specific patient population very  often the patient in our office does not  meet all of those criteria but what we  do is we extrapolate the best possible  information from what's available  applied to this  patience individual clinical scenario  well albeit we're talking about perhaps  a larger scale of patients now we're  talking thinking about all of stage one  but there's a very high percentage of  those stage one patients that we can now  identify as being a very high risk of  death even after complete resection of  their lung tumors and is it ethical to  withhold a potentially life-saving  intervention when we know these patients  are a very high risk of death and have a  very reasonable chance of benefit from  an intervention even though we haven't  yet completed the prospective randomized  study targeting specifically those  high-risk patients that would give an  opportunity for definitive prospective  randomized data or undertaking that  study but in the interim is it really  ethical to leave those patients without  any intervention when there isn't an  unknown intervention that has a very  reasonable supportive body of evidence  that these patients are likely to  benefit and when we talk about benefit  we're talking about potentially saving  their lives thank you the next question  comes in and it's around what is the  significance of the intermediate risk  score very good question we chose to try  for Kate the risk scores when we develop  this assay and its clinical application  rather than just high and low risk  patients to look at high intermediate  and low-risk patients we realize that  clinicians really need to know who among  their early stage patients who among  those stage want patients who among  those stage two patients are really the  patients at highest risk of death  because those are the patients where the  difficult decisions become less  difficult if we could identify the  patients who are really facing a very  poor prognosis a very high chance of  5-year mortality those are patients that  are very are much more likely to benefit  in in very severe need of that  prognostic information in order to make  a truly informed decision so we chose to  try fer Kate the data so that we had a  lot higher level of confidence that  patients whom were identifying as high  risk of death are really different  patients from those who were calling low  risk of death and that intermediate  group gives  that buffer to have an even higher  degree of confidence that high-risk  patients are truly highest risk and  low-risk patients truly have the lowest  risk of those that we can identify with  this assay now the intermediate patients  or patients too and they need help and  they need to make their own decisions We  certainly have no less information than  we did before running the assay and  perhaps now we have a little bit of more  information to guide those decisions as  well because we know those patients are  not in the highest risk group granted  they're not in the lowest risk group  either but that is meaningful  information that we didn't have before  and that information needs to be  funneled into the to the most informed  decision possible for all of those  patients I think a follow-up to that is  does the assay incorporate any clinical  covariance or other clinical data and  its determination of risk so as I  mentioned briefly in discussing the data  from our publications the algorithm and  the risk score generated by this test  depend entirely on the genetic  expression data that we derived from the  from the patie